Task 1: Define Variables in a Playbook
Subtask 1.1: Understanding Variable Types and Scope

Variables in Ansible make playbooks flexible and reusable. Let's start by understanding different ways to define variables.

Step 1: Connect to your control node and create a new directory for this lab.

mkdir ~/lab4-variables
cd ~/lab4-variables

Step 2: Create your first playbook with inline variables.

nano variables-basic.yml

Step 3: Add the following content to demonstrate different variable definition methods:

---
- name: Basic Variables Demo
  hosts: all
  vars:
    # Simple string variables
    package_name: "httpd"
    service_name: "httpd"
    
    # Numeric variables
    port_number: 80
    max_connections: 100
    
    # Boolean variables
    enable_firewall: true
    debug_mode: false
    
    # List variables
    required_packages:
      - httpd
      - firewalld
      - vim
    
    # Dictionary variables
    web_config:
      document_root: "/var/www/html"
      server_admin: "admin@example.com"
      timeout: 300
  
  tasks:
    - name: Display string variable
      debug:
        msg: "Package to install: {{ package_name }}"
    
    - name: Display numeric variable
      debug:
        msg: "Port number: {{ port_number }}"
    
    - name: Display boolean variable
      debug:
        msg: "Firewall enabled: {{ enable_firewall }}"
    
    - name: Display list variable
      debug:
        msg: "Required packages: {{ required_packages }}"
    
    - name: Display dictionary variable
      debug:
        msg: "Document root: {{ web_config.document_root }}"
    
    - name: Loop through list variable
      debug:
        msg: "Installing package: {{ item }}"
      loop: "{{ required_packages }}"

Step 4: Test the playbook to see how variables work.

ansible-playbook -i inventory variables-basic.yml

